WEBVTT

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.320
perfect planet.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:40.460
There is only one planet in the universe, as far as we know, where there is life.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:47.690
The Earth, our home, the perfect planet.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:57.920
Life here is only possible because of a unique balance of natural forces.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:16.980
energy from the sun powers the living world weather systems transport fresh water around

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:28.580
the globe and full ocean currents circulate life's essential nutrients

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.330
This series will reveal how these global forces have worked together

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.490
to create our planet's fragile life support system.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:53.940
These forces is so important that life would never have even started without it.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:22.400
Across the surface of our planet, there are over 1,500 volcanoes.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.050
This is the most active.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.120
Kilauea in Hawaii.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.980
Liquid rock from the planet's hot interior bursts out through fractures.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:19.380
in the earth's crust and it's been flowing for months. Nothing can stand in the lava's way.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:59.720
Volcanoes are certainly destructive, but without these powerful underground forces, there would be no breathable atmosphere, no oceans, no control.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:15.880
volcanoes, but they're vital for all living things on planet Earth. This is Ol Doño Lengai

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:26.410
in Tanzania. It's one of Africa's most active volcanoes and has erupted over 15 times in the

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:27.390
last hundred years.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:41.820
On its northern flank lies Lake Natron, 400 square miles in extent and one of the world's most corrosive bodies of water.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:54.340
The water wells up from deep below, carrying such a concentration of chemicals that it can burn skin.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.340
One animal thrives here.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.250
Kano birds.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.020
Nearly two million lesser flamingos live in East Africa,

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:26.650
and all of them come here to breed.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:37.960
But they can only nest here when the lake's level drops so far

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.810
that in the centre its bed is exposed.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.340
And that rare moment is now.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:57.500
What they know when the conditions are right is a mystery.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.960
But some have flown...

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.130
Thousands of miles to be here.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.590
The flamingos head for the middle of the lake,

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:12.320
where the intense evaporation has created islands of salt.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.420
It's here that the birds build their nests,

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:23.030
over three miles from the lake's edge and safe from land predators,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:28.100
which are unable to cross the surrounding moat of caustic soda.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.980
The single egg is laid on a mount of hardened salt built by the adults.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.090
Its height and insurance against the sudden rise in water level,

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.130
which often happens on Lake Natron.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.960
As the center dries and enlarges,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.860
more and more flamingos arrive to nest on it.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:08.870
In good years, such as now,

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.140
as many as half a million pairs breed here.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.270
After 28 days of incubation,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.920
a chick emerges into Natron's harsh glare.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.170
Here, temperatures can rise to 55 degrees Celsius.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.570
Mother's wing, however, can serve as a sun shield.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.250
Kick is fed a rich liquid made of the lake's barely-digested algae

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.970
and traces of its parents' blood.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:12.250
After a few days, the chick is strong enough to stretch its legs.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:24.740
It then joins a growing crash made up of chicks of different ages.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.300
The need for fresh water will force the infant birds to trek to springs on the lake's edge.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:43.300
A journey they have to make on foot as their flight feathers have yet to develop.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.190
Every day, the adults fly to these springs to feed.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.680
Here on the lake's margins,

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.090
They collect algae that flourish in the volcanic water.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.690
Vital food for the flamingos.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:17.450
It's the algae that turn the flamingos' feathers pink.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.740
It even colours their eyes.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.510
The adults spend most of their day at the springs.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.800
And now their chicks are going to join them there.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.800
No one wants to get left behind.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.980
It's a three-mile march over razor-sharp flakes of soda.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.520
and sticky, caustic mud.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:09.440
A life-threatening trial, especially for the youngest chicks.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.880
The groups merge into one giant creche.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.260
The challenges are just too great.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:26.230
exhaustion overcomes the weakest. There is yet another threat. Marabu stalks. They have long

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:29.980
legs like a flamingo, but a beak like a Roman.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:55.970
sword and they are patient, waiting for an easy target. Presence creates panic among the chicks.

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.870
The smallest avoid being trampled underfoot

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:13.460
by travelling on the outside of the group, exposes them to attack.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.280
Their only defence is to run,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.340
but you have little chance when you're barely ankle-high to your pursuer.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:12.800
stragglers will almost certainly be taken, but there comes a point when every stalk has had its fill.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:28.480
This little chick.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:44.490
is very lucky. At last, the youngsters reach the freshwater springs at the lake's margin to

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:54.590
rejoin their parents. Almost unbelievably, each chick has a unique call that its parent can recognize.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:20.170
It's not just lesser flamingos that depend on volcanoes,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.800
and so do all living things.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:41.660
Over 80% of the Earth's surface is the result of magma bursting up from the molten interior.

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:55.560
This has started 4 billion years ago and it still continues today.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.830
When lava cools, it solidifies into rock

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:09.950
and eventually weathers into land that can support life.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:21.850
All the Galapagos Islands in the Pacific are volcanic,

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:29.760
and Fernandina is the youngest of them and still growing.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:55.460
center, a volcanic crater four miles wide and over 800 meters deep. It seems dormant,

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:58.540
but it was erupting only two weeks ago.

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:10.200
Even so, there are some who depend on the heat from this volcano.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:21.490
The female landiguana, heavy with eggs, approaches the crater's rim.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:29.970
It took her ten days to climb up here, but the toughest part of her journey...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.290
The journey is still ahead.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:35.970
She's going to descend into the crater itself.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:52.230
Frequent tremors make the crater walls unstable.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:07.780
More people have traveled in space than have visited the bottom of Fernandina's crater.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:13.840
It drives her on.

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:23.060
Warm volcanic ash on the crater floor has the perfect temperature for incubating her eggs.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:27.920
There are no clear paths down.

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.860
Any made last year have been destroyed by rock falls.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:57.820
The slope is steep and unstable.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.810
Tumbling rocks quickly become an avalanche.

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:19.400
With the boulders falling down away from her,

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.250
she's not in immediate danger.

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:29.960
But behind her, higher up, two other females...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.700
have also started the descent.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:41.900
And now T is in the firing line.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:20.820
She's safe. Others before her haven't been so lucky, but she's undeterred.

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:53.780
After an eight-hour descent, she reaches the crater floor.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:13.210
find a suitable place to dig her nest. The best sites are already taken and

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:29.860
nesting females are very aggressive and nodding head means

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.320
keep away. Push off.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:42.030
Ignored warnings are met with

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:42.430
force.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:07.180
But she's come a long way, and she's not going to give up now.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:19.780
She, too, starts to dig a hole in which to lay her eggs.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:29.850
That done, she will head back, leaving the volcano to provide...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.820
the warmth that eggs need if they are to hatch.

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:39.700
Sometimes, of course, the volcano erupts

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.600
and destroys not only eggs, but many nesting adults.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:54.950
For the iguanas, the benefits of nesting in the crater

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.880
are so great that it's worth all the risks.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:11.910
Volcanic islands make up just 5% of the planet's land, but they're home to nearly 20% of its

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:23.520
species. Animals often evolve very swiftly on islands, cut off from their mainland relatives

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.310
and faced with the challenges of isolation.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:38.810
This is Wolf, an extinct volcano and one of the most remote islands of the Galapagos,

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:44.760
800 miles from the nearest large landmass and over a million years old.

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:52.320
A finch, a female.

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:57.720
Its ancestors, doubtless, were blown here by storms many centuries ago.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.500
Most finches, they feed on seeds, nectar, and insects.

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:09.050
There's little food of any kind on wolf.

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:15.520
And since they aren't strong enough flyers to leave the island,

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:20.130
they've had to find food from a very unusual source,

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:26.240
depend on another of the island's inhabitants.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:46.800
Nazca boobies every day these seabirds commute between the island and their fishing grounds out in the open ocean

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:58.720
It's time a pair meet they renew their bond and then catch up with the neighbors

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:10.120
Now is the time to hop into action.

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:39.990
They are vampire finches.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:49.260
Little birds have become one of the world's newest species.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:58.740
Cuts the quill of a large flight feather and the blood flows.

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:05.960
The sight of it attracts the attention of others.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:16.770
Blood is very nutritious, worth fighting for.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:33.620
So, with this never-ending supply of food,

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:36.640
the vampire finches can flourish on an island

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:39.220
where otherwise they could not survive.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:49.160
Why the boobies don't object, nobody knows.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:54.980
Perhaps the finches' behavior started

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:58.980
when they helped the boobies to remove parasites from their plumage.

00:26:02.590 --> 00:26:06.750
Maybe the boobies think that the little vampires are still providing a service.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:09.060
Who can tell?

00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:17.710
Volcanic islands don't last forever.

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:23.430
Changes deep in the Earth's crust bring their eruptions to an end,

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:27.140
and the forces of erosion then begin to wear them down.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.420
but the coral around them continues to grow.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:43.730
Sea levels change, and eventually all that is left

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:49.170
is a ring-shaped island of coral rock surrounding a lagoon.

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:08.580
There are hundreds of atolls in tropical seas, all remnants of vanished volcanoes.

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:21.710
This is one of the largest and most pristine, Aldabra, in the Indian Ocean.

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:29.970
20 million years ago, it was an island with a volcanic crater in it.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:48.300
center. Now it's an atoll. Mangroves took root and seabirds came to nest. One land animal in

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:59.780
particular reached here before man, giant tortoise. These creatures can float.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:04.320
and live without food or fresh water for long periods of time.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:12.340
This isolated atoll is the perfect refuge.

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:16.200
Their only enemy is the sun.

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:27.070
Without shade, a tortoise risks being boiled in his shell.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:37.440
So, after feeding in the cool of the morning, and before the sun becomes too crushingly hot,

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:42.200
they have to find cover, all of them.

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:55.600
There is very little vegetation, but caves in the coral rock can provide the shade they so badly need.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.440
He's nearly there.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:10.880
All that is left is a white knuckle ride to the bottom.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:17.000
He's in.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.760
Thousands of others are still out in the sun.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:29.590
This tree could shade a couple of hundred if they pack tight.

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:58.510
Bumper to bumper parking within an hour or so, or must find shade of some sort if they're to survive.

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:07.340
Thanks for watching!

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:41.150
But the future for Aldabra's tortoises is far from certain.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:46.780
The climate is changing, sea levels are rising,

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:52.850
and Aldabra's tortoises may soon have to find a new refuge or drown.

00:31:00.410 --> 00:31:04.100
In some places, volcanoes erupt not in the ocean,

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:12.380
but in the very middle of the continent, North America.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:18.140
And in its very heart, a volcanic hotspot, Yellowstone.

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:29.980
The last major eruption here came to an end about 600...

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.120
150,000 years ago.

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:36.190
But this volcano is only sleeping.

00:31:38.560 --> 00:31:41.770
Not far below ground, there is still magma.

00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:46.100
That heats the groundwater to boiling point.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.960
Come, spout boiling water and steam to...

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:01.990
of 90 meters or so.

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:08.740
Elsewhere, the scalding waters liquify the ground sediments,

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:11.480
creating pots of boiling mud.

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:23.260
And this underground heating has proved a lifelike for some.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:40.450
river otters. They need a constant supply of food if they're to keep warm in these freezing

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:49.880
temperatures. And this close-knit family know exactly where to find it.

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:13.080
Things are so hot that the rivers never freeze, even in the coldest winters.

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:22.480
So, the otter family can fish the year round.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:51.760
Trouble. This coyote knows that in the middle of winter, a thermal river means otters.

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:54.530
Otters mean fish.

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:03.480
The question is, how to steal their catch?

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:29.470
Thing to do...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.600
is to pretend you're not interested

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:36.090
and hope that they wander close enough

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:37.610
for a snatch and run raid.

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:53.130
It seems that cool under fire otters

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:56.570
can outwit a not so wily coyote.

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:12.360
Many animals benefit from the volcanic heat coming up from below.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:21.380
And we do as well, but not nearly as much as we could.

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:28.890
The volcanic heat could provide us with much of the energy that we need.

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:35.420
And then we could stop burning gas and coal that is now changing the climate worldwide.

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:51.260
Deep in the Earth's crust, below both the oceans and the continents,

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:56.200
there are lines of weakness along which rises liquid rock.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:07.520
The Kamchatka Peninsula in the northwest Pacific lies along one of them.

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:29.960
Only a thousand known geysers in the world, and many of them are here in this isolated valley.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:54.670
From superheated water fills the air, but the danger from the scalding pools does little to deter Kamchatka's most famous residents.

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:07.500
In May, when the rest of Kamchatka is still covered by snow,

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:09.910
this valley is free of it.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:14.760
And bears, having just woken up from their winter sleep, know that.

00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:27.510
The grass is fertilized by mineral-rich water spraying from the geeters.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:36.160
Much-needed forage for a large male bear that hasn't eaten for several months.

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:44.300
And he's not the only one.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:59.960
The bears haven't seen one another for seven months since the start of it.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:07.350
winter, and it seems that right now they would prefer to keep it that way.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:20.920
Things should come between a bear and his first meal of the year, and in one place Kamchatka's

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:25.980
underground forces provide its bears with even more nutritious meals.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:37.580
Curral Lake lies within the crater of an immense volcano

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:41.340
that long ago produced one of the greatest eruptions

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:42.900
in the planet's history.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:49.560
The lake is still circled by active vents,

00:38:52.420 --> 00:38:55.600
and mineral-rich ash from their regular eruptions

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:58.350
has made these waters particularly fertile.

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:05.490
Millions of salmon come here from the Pacific Ocean

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:08.110
in order to spawn in these fertile waters,

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:12.370
which will provide their newly hatched young with abundant food.

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:24.060
So, grass soon ceases to be top of the menu for Kamchatka's bears.

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:29.970
By the time spring has turned to summer...

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:37.020
Curral Lake contains the most dense assembly of bears to be found anywhere on Earth.

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:44.880
All hungry for salmon.

00:39:56.710 --> 00:39:59.840
This early in the season, the fish are still strong.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:18.990
And swift seems to work.

00:40:27.640 --> 00:40:29.960
One old bear knows the...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.020
Right now, there is an easier meal to be had.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:40.350
He remembers that if he paddles out into the lake,

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:43.270
away from the shoals of newly arrived salmon,

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:46.910
there's food that doesn't need chasing.

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:09.230
Some of the first fish to arrive have died,

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:14.770
and their bodies can be easily collected from the bottom of the lake.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:25.590
Diving for them at leisure doesn't use many calories.

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:33.660
And he can take his time.

00:41:47.440 --> 00:41:52.160
A couple of weeks later, the salmon are beginning to spawn and are less wary.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:57.820
Now it's worth running.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:20.760
Fish are so abundant that there is plenty for all,

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:24.940
even those with no previous experience.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.100
There are now so many fish, it's difficult to know which one to chase.

00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:59.360
In a single day, a bear can consume 100,000 calories, 50 times more than the average human being does.

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:04.560
And one part is particularly delicious.

00:43:09.900 --> 00:43:14.000
Caviar, 80 calories in every mouthful.

00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:24.960
By supporting huge concentrations of salmon,

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:28.910
mineral ash from coral lakes, volcanoes,

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:33.310
has made life surprisingly easy for these bears.

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:51.670
The planet depends on minerals brought up by volcanoes

00:43:51.670 --> 00:43:54.000
from the Earth's molten core.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:43:59.860
A single ash cloud...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.300
can carry billions of tons of minerals.

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:19.860
As a consequence, the lands surrounding volcanoes

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:22.140
